content.text:
Excellent point! Packing the signature calldata too gives a **16x reduction**: from ~1028 felt252 down to ~62. And since PK, s1, and mul_hint are all 512-element Zq polynomials, they share the same packed representation.

## Revised Design: `PackedPolynomial512`

### The generic type (in `falcon` package)

```cairo
// falcon/src/packing.cairo

#[derive(Drop, Copy, Serde)]
pub struct PackedPolynomial512 {
    pub s0: felt252, pub s1: felt252, ..., pub s28: felt252,
}

impl PackedPolynomial512 {
    /// Pack 512 Zq values into 29 felt252 slots.
    fn from_coeffs(values: Span<Zq>) -> PackedPolynomial512 { ... }
    /// Unpack back to 512 Zq values.
    fn to_coeffs(self: @PackedPolynomial512) -> Array<Zq> { ... }
}
```

This replaces the current `pack_public_key` / `unpack_public_key` with a struct-based API. The existing packing logic (`pack_9`, `unpack_9`, Horner encoding) stays the same internally.

### Packed signature types (in `falcon` package)

```cairo
// falcon/src/types.cairo — new packed variants

#[derive(Drop, Serde)]
pub struct PackedFalconSignature {
    pub s1: PackedPolynomial512,
    pub salt: Array<felt252>,
}

#[derive(Drop, Serde)]
pub struct PackedFalconVerificationHint {
    pub mul_hint: PackedPolynomial512,
}

#[derive(Drop, Serde)]
pub struct PackedFalconSignatureWithHint {
    pub signature: PackedFalconSignature,
    pub hint: PackedFalconVerificationHint,
}
```

### Verify with packed inputs

A new `verify_packed` function that unpacks on the fly:

```cairo
pub fn verify_packed<H, +HashToPoint<H>, +Drop<H>>(
    pk: @PackedPolynomial512,
    sig: PackedFalconSignatureWithHint,
    message: Span<felt252>,
) -> bool {
    let pk_coeffs = pk.to_coeffs();
    let s1_coeffs = sig.signature.s1.to_coeffs();
    let mul_hint_coeffs = sig.hint.mul_hint.to_coeffs();
    let falcon_pk = FalconPublicKey { h_ntt: pk_coeffs };
    let sig_with_hint = FalconSignatureWithHint {
        signature: FalconSignature { s1: s1_coeffs, salt: sig.signature.salt },
        hint: FalconVerificationHint { mul_hint: mul_hint_coeffs },
    };
    verify::<H>(ref falcon_pk, sig_with_hint, message)
}
```

### Storage in falcon_account

Since `falcon` stays pure-Cairo (no `starknet::Store`), the account component handles storage manually:

```cairo
#[storage]
struct Storage {
    // 29 individual felt252 slots for packed PK
    pk_0: felt252, pk_1: felt252, ..., pk_28: felt252,
}

fn read_packed_pk(self: @ComponentState) -> PackedPolynomial512 {
    PackedPolynomial512 { s0: self.pk_0.read(), s1: self.pk_1.read(), ... }
}

fn write_packed_pk(ref self: ComponentState, pk: PackedPolynomial512) {
    self.pk_0.write(pk.s0); self.pk_1.write(pk.s1); ...
}
```

### Calldata size comparison

| Field | Unpacked | Packed |
|-------|----------|--------|
| s1 | 513 felt252 | 29 felt252 |
| salt | ~4 felt252 | ~4 felt252 |
| mul_hint | 513 felt252 | 29 felt252 |
| **Total** | **~1030** | **~62** |

Does this revised design with `PackedPolynomial512` as the generic type look good?

